<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ifni War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1957">1957</a>–<a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ifni War was a conflict of 1957-1958 in which Moroccan irregulars of the Army of Liberation attacked Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, including Ifni and Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. The Spanish were confined to the town of Sidi Ifni until a Franco-Spanish offensive in 1958 restored control in the Sahara. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarfaya">Tarfaya</a> strip to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1958 and Ifni itself in 1969.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ifni-war.jpg" alt="Ifni War" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pablovazram · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa-Territorio-Ifni-57-58.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> ceded the city of Sidi Ifni to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> at the end of the Hispano-Moroccan War in 1860, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wad-ras">Treaty of Wad Ras</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> did not occupy it until 1934. When Morocco regained independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Spain in 1956, it claimed that Spain's remaining possessions were historically and geographically Moroccan territory. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-v-of-morocco">Mohammed V</a> encouraged efforts to recapture the land and personally funded anti-Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>. Violent demonstrations against Spanish rule broke out in Ifni on 10 April 1957, and in June Francisco Franco sent two battalions of the Spanish Legion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laayoune">El Aaiún</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 23 November 1957 a force of 2,000 Moroccans stormed Spanish garrisons and armouries in and around Ifni, after lines of communication had been cut. The Spanish held Sidi Ifni itself but abandoned two outposts, and many others were besieged. At Tiliuín, 60 locally recruited Tiradores de Ifni held off several hundred Moroccans until paratroopers were dropped on 25 November and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a>'s 6th Battalion broke the siege on 3 December. A platoon sent to relieve Telata was ambushed and lost its commander, Captain Ortiz de Zárate, with two dead and fourteen wounded in all.</p><p>Within a fortnight the Moroccans and their tribal allies controlled most of Ifni. They tried to besiege Sidi Ifni, but the town, supplied by the Spanish Navy and protected by trenches and forward outposts, had 7,500 defenders by 9 December. The siege lasted into June 1958. In January 1958 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> reorganised its army units in Spanish territory as the Saharan Liberation Army, which attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laayoune">El Aaiún</a> on 12 January and ambushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> companies at Edchera the next day. In February a Franco-Spanish offensive, with about 9,000 Spanish and 5,000 French troops and 150 aircraft, broke up the Liberation Army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 2 April 1958 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> signed the Treaty of Angra de Cintra. Morocco obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarfaya">Tarfaya</a>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-juby">Cape Juby</a>, and the Ifni territory, excluding Spanish Sahara, though legally it gained full control of Ifni only in 1969. Spain kept Sidi Ifni until 1969, when it returned it under international pressure, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> resolution 2072 of 1965. It held Spanish Sahara until 1975, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/green-march">Green March</a> led it to sign the Madrid Accords with Morocco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ifni War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1957 to 1958 Moroccan irregulars of the Army of Liberation attacked in the <b>Ifni War</b>, confining Spain to the town itself, which became a Spanish province in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/ifni">Ifni</a></li></ul></section>
